    "q": "Once a prompt is tokenized, what is the primary function of a base (foundation) LLM?",
    "options": [
     "To predict the next token in the sequence",
     "To search the internet",
     "To check facts against a database",
     "To translate the prompt into code"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explain": "Base models complete text token by token; instruction tuning makes them follow requests.",
    "lesson": "prompt_eng-01",
    "evidence": "the primary function of the \"Base LLM\" (or Foundation model) is to predict the token in that sequence.",
    "origin": "authored"
   },
   {
    "q": "Besides giving examples, which technique uses primary content to steer the model?",
    "options": [
     "Raising the temperature",
     "Asking the same question twice",
     "Providing cues rather than examples",
     "Writing the prompt in capital letters"
    ],
    "answer": 2,
    "explain": "A cue (for example the start of the desired output) nudges the model toward the format you want.",
    "lesson": "prompt_eng-02",
    "evidence": "Another technique for using primary content is to provide cues rather than examples.",
    "origin": "authored"
   },
   {
    "q": "What does the quality of results from a prompt depend on?",
    "options": [
     "Only on which model you use",
     "How much information you provide and how well-crafted the prompt is",
     "The time of day",
     "How long you wait before sending it"
